WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.780
- So, I'm making bagels from scratch.

00:00:01.780 --> 00:00:04.445
I've only made 200 so far.
(laughing)

00:00:04.445 --> 00:00:07.028
(upbeat music)

00:00:08.660 --> 00:00:09.493
Hi, guys.

00:00:09.493 --> 00:00:10.610
I'm Matt, a Tasty producer,

00:00:10.610 --> 00:00:11.810
and today I wanna find out

00:00:11.810 --> 00:00:13.780
if it's worth making your own bagels at home

00:00:13.780 --> 00:00:15.510
or if you should just buy them from the store.

00:00:15.510 --> 00:00:17.743
^So, I'm gonna prep my ingredients and get started.

00:00:20.920 --> 00:00:23.150
I buy most of bagels from the grocery store,

00:00:23.150 --> 00:00:24.610
so for this first comparison,

00:00:24.610 --> 00:00:26.753
I've decided to use the brand that I usually get.

00:00:26.753 --> 00:00:29.015
It's pre-sliced and consistently good,

00:00:29.015 --> 00:00:31.145
which is really all you can ask for

00:00:31.145 --> 00:00:32.853
from a pre-packaged bagel.

00:00:37.040 --> 00:00:39.860
My second comparison bagel comes from a well-rated bakery

00:00:39.860 --> 00:00:41.530
here in Los Angeles, and yeah,

00:00:41.530 --> 00:00:43.790
I know New York has some of the best bagels in the world.

00:00:43.790 --> 00:00:45.450
Luckily, most of the reviews said

00:00:45.450 --> 00:00:47.060
that these were the closest thing they'd had

00:00:47.060 --> 00:00:49.383
to a New York style bagel outside of New York,

00:00:49.540 --> 00:00:51.530
and honestly, I kind of agree with them.

00:00:51.530 --> 00:00:53.310
They were made fresh the day I bought them

00:00:53.310 --> 00:00:55.060
and they looked and tasted amazing.

00:00:55.210 --> 00:00:57.740
^This homemade recipe was developed by our food coordinator,

00:00:57.740 --> 00:01:01.087
^Alexis, and tested many many times by both of us,

00:01:01.087 --> 00:01:03.590
and I mean so many times.

00:01:03.590 --> 00:01:05.933
You have no idea how many bagels I made.

00:01:06.460 --> 00:01:08.823
Bagels are deceptively simple looking.

00:01:08.980 --> 00:01:10.893
Sure, they don't require that many ingredients,

00:01:10.893 --> 00:01:13.683
but there are tons of ways for things to go wrong,

00:01:13.820 --> 00:01:16.280
and I know because I messed up so many times.

00:01:16.280 --> 00:01:20.000
Speaking of mistakes, you cannot substitute for bread flour.

00:01:20.000 --> 00:01:21.780
You need that nice gluten development

00:01:21.780 --> 00:01:23.973
to get the right consistency and texture.

00:01:24.340 --> 00:01:27.120
Because of that, do not consider kneading the dough by hand.

00:01:27.120 --> 00:01:28.897
The quickest way is with a food processor.

00:01:28.897 --> 00:01:30.990
If you try to knead it with your hands,

00:01:30.990 --> 00:01:32.510
it'll take close to a half hour

00:01:32.510 --> 00:01:34.060
^to reach the right consistency.

00:01:34.400 --> 00:01:35.650
^You're not making bread,

00:01:35.650 --> 00:01:37.800
so the dough should not be moist and gooey,

00:01:37.800 --> 00:01:38.970
it should be a bit firm

00:01:38.970 --> 00:01:41.273
and very very stretchy like a rubber band.

00:01:41.460 --> 00:01:42.350
But don't over-knead

00:01:42.350 --> 00:01:44.663
or you'll end up with really dense braids.

00:01:44.920 --> 00:01:48.053
Oiling is how bagels get their shine and chewy texture.

00:01:48.470 --> 00:01:51.693
Now, I used barley malt syrup for color and flavor.

00:01:53.350 --> 00:01:54.630
The water tray is optional,

00:01:54.630 --> 00:01:56.240
but I found that whenever I used it

00:01:56.240 --> 00:01:59.033
my bagels cooked more evenly and didn't burn as badly.

00:02:04.910 --> 00:02:05.810
Once your dough is ready,

00:02:05.810 --> 00:02:08.217
feel free to divide it into six or eight pieces,

00:02:08.217 --> 00:02:10.473
keeping them as uniform as possible.

00:02:10.650 --> 00:02:11.520
You can use a scale

00:02:11.520 --> 00:02:14.320
if you really wanna be specific about the sizes of them.

00:02:14.820 --> 00:02:17.490
Now, this is not the official bagel forming technique

00:02:17.490 --> 00:02:18.620
that professionals use.

00:02:18.620 --> 00:02:20.760
So, this method is what worked best for me

00:02:20.760 --> 00:02:22.200
given the constraints that I had.

00:02:22.200 --> 00:02:23.428
You're basically working the dough

00:02:23.428 --> 00:02:25.460
so that the surface stretches tight

00:02:25.460 --> 00:02:27.860
and the seam on the bottom gets as small as possible.

00:02:27.860 --> 00:02:31.040
Poke a whole through it and gently stretch the ring out,

00:02:31.040 --> 00:02:32.103
rotating as you go.

00:02:33.000 --> 00:02:34.603
Make sure to take your time.

00:02:34.650 --> 00:02:35.730
If you go too quickly,

00:02:35.730 --> 00:02:38.480
you'll tear the dough and it'll burst open in the oven.

00:02:38.660 --> 00:02:41.733
And I'm gonna be real, this step is exhausting.

00:02:41.750 --> 00:02:43.532
It's a real arm workout, but persevere,

00:02:43.532 --> 00:02:45.603
it's totally worth it in the end.

00:02:52.340 --> 00:02:55.283
Now, I boil my bagels for about 30 seconds a side;

00:02:55.440 --> 00:02:57.390
^however, if you want chewier bagels,

00:02:57.390 --> 00:02:59.343
^you can let them go for up to a minute.

00:02:59.600 --> 00:03:02.203
^Just don't go for too long or they'll overcook.

00:03:11.640 --> 00:03:13.180
An egg wash is optional,

00:03:13.180 --> 00:03:14.910
but it guarantees the bagels will be shiny

00:03:14.910 --> 00:03:16.510
and golden brown on the outside.

00:03:16.720 --> 00:03:18.970
Plus, it's how you get the toppings to stick,

00:03:18.977 --> 00:03:20.930
and you can use whatever toppings you want.

00:03:20.930 --> 00:03:23.261
Feel free to experiment, have a little bit of fun.

00:03:23.261 --> 00:03:26.293
Sesame and poppy seeds are good enough for me though.

00:03:26.970 --> 00:03:28.813
And that's it, you've made bagels.

00:03:31.640 --> 00:03:33.310
They're best if you eat them right away.

00:03:33.310 --> 00:03:35.503
Within a day, they'll get pretty stale.

00:03:35.690 --> 00:03:37.970
It's a lot of work, but if everything goes right,

00:03:37.970 --> 00:03:40.570
I promise you'll be proud of the bagels you've made.

00:03:46.180 --> 00:03:47.840
So, how did the bagels stack up?

00:03:47.840 --> 00:03:49.740
^The store bought bagels are the cheapest,

00:03:49.740 --> 00:03:51.793
^but barely beat out the bakery ones.

00:03:51.880 --> 00:03:53.550
^The homemade ones are the most expensive,

00:03:53.550 --> 00:03:55.533
which makes sense given the cost of the syrup,

00:03:55.533 --> 00:03:57.330
the yeast, and all the ingredients.

00:03:57.330 --> 00:03:58.910
^The store bought and bakery style bagels

00:03:58.910 --> 00:04:00.650
^are also the most time efficient,

00:04:00.650 --> 00:04:02.550
^taking a matter of minutes to prepare,

00:04:02.550 --> 00:04:04.541
^while the homemade ones take hours.

00:04:04.541 --> 00:04:07.960
The store bought and bakery are also the least challenging.

00:04:07.960 --> 00:04:09.993
All you have to do is toast and top.

00:04:10.220 --> 00:04:13.450
It took 16 practice batches for me to feel confident

00:04:13.450 --> 00:04:14.953
making bagels from scratch.

00:04:15.510 --> 00:04:16.990
The store bought and bakery bagels

00:04:16.990 --> 00:04:19.253
are much easier to clean up after as well.

00:04:19.550 --> 00:04:21.210
Homemade bagels require mountains

00:04:21.210 --> 00:04:23.310
of equipment and cookware that need cleaning,

00:04:23.310 --> 00:04:25.603
but ultimately, it all comes down to taste.

00:04:25.860 --> 00:04:27.030
In a blind taste test,

00:04:27.030 --> 00:04:29.762
my coworkers favored the bakery and homemade bagels

00:04:29.762 --> 00:04:32.303
for their balance of flavor and texture.

00:04:32.510 --> 00:04:35.393
So, should you make your own bagels at home?

00:04:35.506 --> 00:04:38.250
No, absolutely not.
(record scratching)

00:04:38.250 --> 00:04:41.253
If you like a challenge, by all means go for it.

00:04:41.320 --> 00:04:43.360
I'm proud of myself for tackling this,

00:04:43.360 --> 00:04:46.370
but they take way too much time and effort

00:04:46.370 --> 00:04:48.673
to justify making on a regular basis.

00:04:48.880 --> 00:04:49.837
Store bought is great,

00:04:49.837 --> 00:04:52.067
it's convenient and consistently good,

00:04:52.067 --> 00:04:54.653
but nothing beats a bagel from a bakery.

00:04:55.070 --> 00:04:59.223
They're fresh and chewy and delicious, and by buying them,

00:04:59.250 --> 00:05:01.250
you're helping support a local business,

00:05:01.510 --> 00:05:03.410
and I think that's the best way to go.

00:05:03.650 --> 00:05:06.123
Eeny, meeny, miny, moe, that one.

00:05:07.777 --> 00:05:10.360
(upbeat music)

